All nine returned `PKIStatus: granted` and echoed the request nonce exactly.
No TSA refused the request; no rate limit was hit; one request per endpoint,
spaced >= 3 s (Sectigo's ~15 s spacing caveat and SwissSign's ~10/day quota
were not approached).

**`timestamp.entrust.net` is served by Sectigo.** Its token carries the same
signer certificate (issuer `CN=Sectigo Public Time Stamping CA R41`, serial
`0xE74EF255B0504FFADBA6DFF7FC8BA315`) as `timestamp.sectigo.com`. The two are
one TSA behind two names — they are **not** two independent anchors.

## Derived tamper fixtures (generated locally from the captures above)

| file | derived from | mutation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D60-ber-indefinite-freetsa.tsr      | D60-tsa-freetsa-resp.tsr  | outer 3 nesting levels re-encoded with BER indefinite lengths + EOC |
| D60-ber-indefinite-digicert.tsr     | D60-tsa-digicert-resp.tsr | same |
| D60-ber-nonminimal-len-freetsa.tsr  | D60-tsa-freetsa-resp.tsr  | outer SEQUENCE length re-encoded as a non-minimal 4-octet long form (`30 84 00 00 12 1f`) |
| D60-ber-nonminimal-len-digicert.tsr | D60-tsa-digicert-resp.tsr | same |
| D60-ber-unsorted-setof-freetsa.tsr  | D60-tsa-freetsa-resp.tsr  | the four `signedAttrs` reversed in place (same length, non-DER SET OF order) |

The two indefinite-length fixtures are **valid BER** — `openssl ts -reply`
parses them and `der 0.8.1`'s `Decode::from_ber` accepts the FreeTSA one.
That is what makes them a real BER-where-DER-required case rather than
garbage: a parser that rejects them must be rejecting them *for being BER*.
